WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.150 --> 00:00:04.590
You aren't very far along, but
you have that sense of confidence.

00:00:04.890 --> 00:00:06.210
I can do this language.

00:00:06.330 --> 00:00:09.630
Hi there, Steve Kaufmann, and
today I wanna talk about how do

00:00:09.630 --> 00:00:11.340
you get started in a language.

00:00:11.700 --> 00:00:14.850
Uh, remember, if you enjoy
these videos, please subscribe.

00:00:14.855 --> 00:00:18.150
Click on the bell for notifications,
uh, so you'll know when a

00:00:18.150 --> 00:00:19.560
new video has been published.

00:00:19.950 --> 00:00:23.100
And if you follow me on a podcast
service, please leave a comment.

00:00:23.250 --> 00:00:26.430
So I often get asked the question like,
how do you get started in the language?

00:00:26.460 --> 00:00:30.330
And you know, here I am, you know, 77.

00:00:30.720 --> 00:00:32.950
In the last 17 years I've
learned 10 languages.

00:00:32.950 --> 00:00:34.830
So I have started a lot of languages.

00:00:34.890 --> 00:00:39.900
And of course we say that the key to
language learning is comprehensible input.

00:00:40.140 --> 00:00:43.230
But when you start in a language,
nothing is comprehensible.

00:00:43.739 --> 00:00:46.989
So then how do we get a
toehold in the language?

00:00:46.995 --> 00:00:51.105
So I, I would like to share with you
uh, sort of my experience and, and even

00:00:51.105 --> 00:00:55.305
with languages that I've learned in the
last, say, 15 years or so, which have,

00:00:55.665 --> 00:00:59.834
you know, different writing systems
like, uh, Greek or, uh, Ukrainian

00:00:59.834 --> 00:01:05.804
or Arabic or, uh, you know, Korean
or uh, Persian and stuff like that.

00:01:05.804 --> 00:01:06.854
Very difficult.

00:01:06.914 --> 00:01:10.875
Particularly if the writing system is,
is is different from what you're used to.

00:01:11.235 --> 00:01:14.820
You know, the Latin alphabet
that we spent, our whole lives,

00:01:14.910 --> 00:01:18.510
uh, reading in where it's, it's
basically second nature for us.

00:01:18.990 --> 00:01:19.890
So how do you get started?

00:01:20.310 --> 00:01:21.450
So, a few concepts.

00:01:21.480 --> 00:01:24.600
First of all, believe you're
gonna get there, okay?

00:01:24.960 --> 00:01:26.910
It seems almost impossible.

00:01:26.910 --> 00:01:31.950
It's like looking at a, you've got a, a
jigsaw puzzle and there's nothing there.

00:01:31.980 --> 00:01:36.630
There you've got a bunch of little
tiles, funny shapes, and, and

00:01:36.630 --> 00:01:38.070
yet, you know there's a picture.

00:01:39.089 --> 00:01:40.350
You know, there's a picture there.

00:01:40.740 --> 00:01:45.000
And, and, and you know, from experience,
if you've handled jigsaw puzzles

00:01:45.000 --> 00:01:50.399
before that gradually as you put more
and more pieces on the table, you're

00:01:50.399 --> 00:01:52.530
gonna see how they connect together.

00:01:52.530 --> 00:01:55.679
And over time, uh, you're
gonna get a full picture.

00:01:55.679 --> 00:01:57.780
But when you start out,
it seems impossible.

00:01:57.899 --> 00:02:02.340
But it's, it's, it's very, you know,
important to have that feeling that

00:02:02.669 --> 00:02:08.370
in time, with enough time and if I
remain motivated then I will get there.

00:02:08.580 --> 00:02:08.850
Okay.

00:02:08.850 --> 00:02:12.420
Because at first it seems impossible,
particularly when I had to try to

00:02:12.420 --> 00:02:14.370
learn the Arabic writing system.

00:02:14.520 --> 00:02:18.870
I, I don't remember 50 years ago
when I learned Chinese, but for, um,

00:02:18.960 --> 00:02:22.860
Arabic and Persian, I had to decipher
this, this writing system where the

00:02:22.860 --> 00:02:26.640
characters very, you know, depending
on whether it's the beginning of a, of

00:02:26.640 --> 00:02:29.160
a word, the middle of the word, or the
end of the word, like there's lots of

00:02:29.160 --> 00:02:30.970
complication, but you have to believe.

00:02:31.630 --> 00:02:33.315
The next thing is repetition.

00:02:33.435 --> 00:02:37.755
You know, as, as I often quote Manfred
Spitzer who said, You know, uh, to learn

00:02:37.755 --> 00:02:41.204
things, we need two things: we need
repetition, and we, we need novelty.

00:02:41.265 --> 00:02:41.595
Okay?

00:02:41.894 --> 00:02:44.385
The language is new, but
it's incomprehensible.

00:02:44.745 --> 00:02:47.834
So there's not that rewarding
kind of novelty because you

00:02:47.834 --> 00:02:49.095
don't understand what's there.

00:02:49.575 --> 00:02:50.865
So you need repetition.

00:02:50.865 --> 00:02:52.665
You need to get the neurons to fire.

00:02:53.475 --> 00:02:57.435
So you have to do a lot of repetitive
reading and listening lots.

00:02:57.855 --> 00:03:01.515
Uh, I often mention that I
start with the mini stories.

00:03:01.860 --> 00:03:05.400
Even though I'm, in a way, it's, this
is, it's not like, Hello, how are you?

00:03:05.400 --> 00:03:06.870
My name is, How old are you?

00:03:07.080 --> 00:03:08.190
Not that kind of stuff.

00:03:08.190 --> 00:03:12.960
It's actually relatively simple
stories where there's a lot of

00:03:12.960 --> 00:03:14.910
repetition within each story.

00:03:14.915 --> 00:03:19.020
Most vocabulary repeats five times in a
different tense or a different person.

00:03:19.800 --> 00:03:25.340
Uh, and so I go through, typically in
sentence mode in LingQ, and I look up

00:03:25.340 --> 00:03:27.140
the words and I listen to the wor...

00:03:27.140 --> 00:03:31.010
the sentence, and then I eventually
get through that whole lesson,

00:03:31.010 --> 00:03:32.570
five minutes worth of a lesson.

00:03:32.780 --> 00:03:33.920
I listen to it again.

00:03:34.010 --> 00:03:34.940
I don't wait.

00:03:34.940 --> 00:03:37.790
And I think this is another point, I
don't wait till I fully understand it,

00:03:37.790 --> 00:03:39.500
because I'm not going to understand it.

00:03:39.740 --> 00:03:42.680
When you're starting into it, even if
you go through it and you look up every

00:03:42.680 --> 00:03:45.420
word and you went through the sentence
and you'd think you know what the

00:03:45.420 --> 00:03:48.179
sentence means, but when you hear it
again, you say, I can't understand it.

00:03:48.480 --> 00:03:53.340
So the idea that you're gonna
ace lesson one before you move

00:03:53.340 --> 00:03:55.170
to lesson two is not a good idea.

00:03:55.649 --> 00:03:59.609
I typically, as I say, I use the
mini stories I go lesson one.

00:03:59.609 --> 00:04:01.290
I get a bit of a sense of it.

00:04:01.500 --> 00:04:01.859
Now.

00:04:01.859 --> 00:04:06.329
This desire for novelty takes over even
though I don't understand lesson one.

00:04:06.480 --> 00:04:09.870
I wanna go to lesson two
becasue I'm tired of, you know,

00:04:10.480 --> 00:04:13.620
uh, beating my head against a
brick wall with lesson one and not

00:04:13.620 --> 00:04:15.360
understanding, so I go to lesson two.

00:04:15.780 --> 00:04:16.470
New stuff.

00:04:16.530 --> 00:04:17.160
New story.

00:04:17.190 --> 00:04:17.700
Okay.

00:04:17.940 --> 00:04:19.140
I have the translation there.

00:04:19.140 --> 00:04:20.130
I can look up words.

00:04:20.130 --> 00:04:23.850
I'm kind of listening to
it, and you just keep going.

00:04:23.850 --> 00:04:26.490
And then at some point you
go back to lesson one again.

00:04:26.910 --> 00:04:32.490
And on that basis, typically I'll notice
that in my statistics at LingQ, I will

00:04:32.490 --> 00:04:37.350
listen to particularly the first 10 or
15 stories - we have 60 mini stories.

00:04:37.560 --> 00:04:41.565
I'll listen to them 30 or 40
times  but not at one sitting.

00:04:41.565 --> 00:04:47.085
It's very important to not push yourself
too hard to try to learn this now.

00:04:47.114 --> 00:04:49.695
You have to recognize that the
brain doesn't learn that way.

00:04:49.935 --> 00:04:53.145
The learn the brain wants
the novelty, wants to mean or

00:04:53.150 --> 00:04:54.564
wants to go onto new things.

00:04:54.564 --> 00:04:56.655
And then you can come back to it later on.

00:04:56.895 --> 00:04:59.984
Don't, you know, try to ace things.

00:05:00.734 --> 00:05:04.724
And another advice I would
have is listen before reading.

00:05:05.115 --> 00:05:08.505
And the reason for that is when
you listen, of course you don't

00:05:08.505 --> 00:05:12.705
understand but you've now got
a certain degree of curiosity.

00:05:12.795 --> 00:05:14.445
Like, what was that?

00:05:15.045 --> 00:05:18.375
And, and this is true at the
beginning when it's all just noise.

00:05:18.735 --> 00:05:23.625
But even later on as you progress in the
language, you start to hear where words

00:05:23.835 --> 00:05:28.305
end and begin, even though you don't know
those words, you get a, you know, as the

00:05:28.305 --> 00:05:33.075
jigsaw puzzle starts to fill out, you
get a better sense of the words there.

00:05:33.135 --> 00:05:37.544
And so you start to become, you know,
curious and curious is a tr curiosity is a

00:05:37.544 --> 00:05:40.275
tremendous driver, a tremendous motivator.

00:05:40.484 --> 00:05:45.974
So by listening first you, you increase
your level of curiosity in that text,

00:05:46.275 --> 00:05:50.955
you've also got a bit of momentum so that
when you go to read, and particularly

00:05:50.955 --> 00:05:54.645
if you're reading, you know, in sentence
mode and you listen to that sentence,

00:05:54.645 --> 00:05:57.195
you can hear the audio for that
sentence and you've heard it before.

00:05:57.435 --> 00:06:01.695
All of that is building up some
momentum because when you are reading,

00:06:01.965 --> 00:06:03.455
you're gonna be sub-vocalizing.

00:06:03.469 --> 00:06:04.034
You have to...

00:06:04.545 --> 00:06:07.965
the, the whatever you're reading
represents the sound, so you

00:06:07.969 --> 00:06:11.355
have to have a sense of what that
sound is, or at least some of it.

00:06:11.534 --> 00:06:15.205
So I very much recommend listening
even though you don't understand.

00:06:15.540 --> 00:06:19.560
But then getting right in there to try
and save words, to get some sense of what

00:06:19.560 --> 00:06:25.560
it's about, and then you listen again
and it's this repetitive activity of, of

00:06:25.560 --> 00:06:29.550
listening and reading, uh, and going over
the same material over and over again.

00:06:29.555 --> 00:06:31.980
That's gonna be a big part of,
I would say the first three

00:06:31.980 --> 00:06:33.120
months of your language learning.

00:06:34.050 --> 00:06:38.670
Uh, the first three months,
uh, is somewhat satisfying.

00:06:39.525 --> 00:06:42.465
It doesn't matter what you can say,
day one, you know "my name is...".

00:06:42.645 --> 00:06:43.485
That doesn't really matter.

00:06:43.665 --> 00:06:46.545
What matters is are you
starting to understand more.

00:06:46.605 --> 00:06:47.594
Is your brain start...

00:06:47.594 --> 00:06:49.635
brain starting to get
used to the language?

00:06:49.844 --> 00:06:53.445
And the first three months, if you
do a lot of repetitive listening, I

00:06:53.445 --> 00:06:57.495
find that I have a lot of tolerance
for repetitive listening and reading

00:06:57.615 --> 00:07:01.395
in the early stage in the language,
because the language is so new to me.

00:07:01.664 --> 00:07:06.015
So I am combining that repetitive,
repetitive, you know, the repetition

00:07:06.210 --> 00:07:08.550
with the sense that the language is new.

00:07:08.550 --> 00:07:09.840
So there's some novelty there.

00:07:09.900 --> 00:07:10.710
I'm curious.

00:07:10.770 --> 00:07:12.510
I want to get to know the language better.

00:07:12.510 --> 00:07:13.590
How does the language work?

00:07:13.590 --> 00:07:15.990
And so that drives me through
that first three month period.

00:07:16.530 --> 00:07:20.520
And, and that is a, if you can get
to where you have a feeling that

00:07:20.520 --> 00:07:24.240
you made significant progress in
the first three months, it's not

00:07:24.240 --> 00:07:25.560
that you understand everything.

00:07:25.770 --> 00:07:28.230
Lots of the, what you listen
to, you don't understand.

00:07:28.230 --> 00:07:29.430
You keep forgetting the words.

00:07:29.430 --> 00:07:30.560
You can't really say much.

00:07:31.000 --> 00:07:35.460
You aren't very far along, but
you have that sense of confidence.

00:07:35.730 --> 00:07:38.250
I can do this language, and that...

00:07:38.280 --> 00:07:40.380
That's where the first
three months are so crucial.

00:07:40.650 --> 00:07:43.440
You have to push yourself in
that first three months to

00:07:43.440 --> 00:07:45.150
get to where, you know what?

00:07:45.150 --> 00:07:48.960
I knew nothing about the language
when I started and now look at me.

00:07:48.960 --> 00:07:53.200
I can actually understand some
things, and that is, in my

00:07:53.205 --> 00:07:54.480
experience, tremendously motivating.

00:07:54.780 --> 00:07:59.740
Now you can start crawling out from
that and exploring other things.

00:07:59.800 --> 00:08:04.420
I should also point out that I
generally like to buy a starter book

00:08:04.450 --> 00:08:07.300
to go with my, uh, activity at LingQ.

00:08:07.450 --> 00:08:11.800
So it can be a Teach Yourself, it can be
a colloquial, there's a, and and if your

00:08:11.805 --> 00:08:14.830
native language is not English, I'm sure
there are starter books from whatev...

00:08:14.830 --> 00:08:18.050
for whatever language you're trying
to learn based on your language.

00:08:18.050 --> 00:08:19.780
It gives you an overview of the grammar.

00:08:20.050 --> 00:08:23.920
Yes, you can also Google and download
grammars, but there's something to holding

00:08:23.920 --> 00:08:25.780
onto a book, flipping through a book.

00:08:25.810 --> 00:08:29.610
You sit down in a chair, you're looking
through a book, it's very useful to

00:08:29.610 --> 00:08:35.505
have that companion starter book, and
it's something you look back to with

00:08:35.505 --> 00:08:40.095
nostalgia when a year or two later
you're quite good in the language.

00:08:40.155 --> 00:08:44.295
It's nice to look back at that
starter book that got you started,

00:08:44.685 --> 00:08:46.665
um, and it sits on your shelf.

00:08:46.670 --> 00:08:49.064
And even though I don't need
it anymore, I keep it there.

00:08:49.185 --> 00:08:51.464
So that's some advice.

00:08:51.470 --> 00:08:52.694
It's not the whole story.

00:08:53.115 --> 00:08:55.765
It's some advice on how to
get started in a language.

00:08:55.875 --> 00:08:57.975
And I look forward to your questions.

00:08:58.485 --> 00:08:59.444
Thank you for listening.

00:08:59.605 --> 00:09:00.084
Bye for now.

